WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.220
And so the listening is sort of
the preparation for speaking.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.390
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here and today
I want to talk a little bit about

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:15.300
listening, why you should listen before
you speak in language learning, and

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.620
maybe that's the lesson for life.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.370
Make sure your mind is open and
that you're listening before you

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.260
just say whatever you want to say.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:26.850
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.150
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.730
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:39.450
So if you follow me here, you know that
I believe in the power of input, uh, I

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:44.910
believe in Stephen Krashen, uh, that we
learn from meaningful messages and we

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.210
learn when we're listening to a language
and we hear things that are somehow

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.000
compelling for us, that way we learn.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:56.760
And, um, of course in history,
long before we had, so input can

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.020
be listening and it can be reading.

00:00:58.605 --> 00:01:01.665
Those are the two ways in which
the language, which we don't have

00:01:01.665 --> 00:01:03.855
inside us, can come into our brain.

00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:09.555
We can develop new language habits based
on this stimulus from the language that

00:01:09.555 --> 00:01:11.595
we receive in listening or reading.

00:01:12.255 --> 00:01:18.795
And, uh, but listening, you know,
obviously historically precedes reading,

00:01:19.335 --> 00:01:25.455
uh, humans were communicating orally
long before we had any writing system.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:31.930
People learn things by listening
to the elders or listening

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.660
to experts and so forth.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.720
So listening is, has been
around for a long time.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.240
So how do we listen to learn languages?

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:45.610
Um, in my own case, listening
is my favorite activity

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.440
because it's so easy to do.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.520
And I think that's important.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.610
We kind of force ourselves to do things
all the time that we find difficult.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:56.759
We don't want to be pushing against,
you know, too much resistance.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:01:59.699
Listening is something that I
can do first thing in the morning

00:01:59.699 --> 00:02:03.300
while doing my exercises, I can
do it while preparing breakfast.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.710
I can do it while in the car.

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:09.449
And I do a lot of it, probably more
than any other activity, but listening

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:14.459
implies some degree of understanding or
at least an attempt at understanding.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.260
Otherwise we just hear something.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.209
So we just hear noise.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.500
We just hear a language and it's,
there's nothing there that we understand.

00:02:23.579 --> 00:02:25.380
I don't think that's very
effective for language learning.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:30.209
So I always make a point of listening
to something where I have access

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:34.350
to a transcript at a very early
stage in my language learning.

00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:38.880
Uh, I rely on going over the transcript
because it is just noise when I start

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:42.969
and I go through the transcript, I
might look up words in LingQ and I

00:02:42.969 --> 00:02:47.970
review the words and I listen again,
and I listen to the whole lesson again

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.510
and gradually sort of the fog lifts.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.980
It's like the jigsaw puzzle as more
pieces fall into place we start to

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.920
notice some other things, but always the
listening is the easiest thing to do.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:09.720
And it also triggers my reading where
I find it easier to sort of define

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.739
the words that I'm hearing if I just
rely on my listening, I, I can't

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:17.475
really grab what those words are.

00:03:17.505 --> 00:03:21.165
So it's very much a combination
of listening and reading, but most

00:03:21.165 --> 00:03:22.935
of the time is spent listening.

00:03:23.894 --> 00:03:29.234
Now, when we listen, we aren't,
and here, I want to point out some

00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:30.765
things that I think experienced...

00:03:31.255 --> 00:03:35.745
at least I shouldn't say that, but in
my own case, I know that I will continue

00:03:35.774 --> 00:03:37.845
not understanding for a long time.

00:03:37.964 --> 00:03:39.135
It doesn't bother me.

00:03:39.644 --> 00:03:44.524
So we listen, we don't fully understand,
but we are understanding something.

00:03:45.285 --> 00:03:47.325
And so the language is washing over us.

00:03:47.385 --> 00:03:48.225
We are listening.

00:03:48.225 --> 00:03:49.905
We're not just hearing the language.

00:03:50.025 --> 00:03:50.745
We are listening.

00:03:50.745 --> 00:03:52.395
We're grabbing things here and there.

00:03:52.575 --> 00:03:55.725
And when we listen to the second
time we notice some other things.

00:03:56.115 --> 00:04:01.935
So, um, I combine it with listening,
but the listening itself, which is

00:04:01.935 --> 00:04:06.855
so easy to do is also helping the
language to wash over my brain.

00:04:07.155 --> 00:04:09.885
And I do pick out certain
things every time I listen.

00:04:10.515 --> 00:04:15.375
Um, now the other thing about listening,
I like listening while I'm doing other

00:04:15.375 --> 00:04:20.325
tasks, because I actually surprisingly
find it easier to concentrate...

00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:24.645
start off by saying we can't concentrate
a hundred percent when we are listening

00:04:24.645 --> 00:04:29.685
to anything, uh, let alone in a foreign
language where it requires a little more

00:04:29.685 --> 00:04:34.965
concentration to, to understand what's
being said to sort of seize upon those

00:04:34.965 --> 00:04:38.925
things that we do understand and try to
fill in some things that we don't yet

00:04:38.925 --> 00:04:40.335
understand and guess what they mean.

00:04:41.295 --> 00:04:44.175
So all of this stuff requires a fair
amount of concentration, but you

00:04:44.175 --> 00:04:46.425
can't be concentrating the whole time.

00:04:47.595 --> 00:04:51.675
If I sit down to deliberately
listen, I find it more difficult.

00:04:51.705 --> 00:04:54.675
My mind is more likely to
wander for some reason.

00:04:55.395 --> 00:05:01.275
On the other hand, if I'm driving, if I'm
doing the dishes, if I'm exercising, I'm

00:05:01.275 --> 00:05:03.675
not worried about when my mind wanders.

00:05:03.675 --> 00:05:08.025
I'm assuming that my mind is going
to wander uh, and for some reason

00:05:08.025 --> 00:05:09.135
I'm able to better concentrate.

00:05:09.165 --> 00:05:12.225
Obviously exercising is more
difficult, but doing the dishes I'm

00:05:12.225 --> 00:05:13.905
able to, I'm able to concentrate.

00:05:14.265 --> 00:05:17.925
And if it's, if it's the mini stories
where there's a lot of repetition,

00:05:18.345 --> 00:05:21.015
I start noticing certain structures
that I didn't notice before.

00:05:21.345 --> 00:05:26.085
If it's more advanced content in
languages that I speak say relatively

00:05:26.085 --> 00:05:29.385
well, I'm more focused on the meaning.

00:05:29.785 --> 00:05:34.094
I don't listen to those things so
often, but all of this has helped the

00:05:34.094 --> 00:05:36.525
language sort of wash over my brain.

00:05:37.215 --> 00:05:43.125
And while we do this, and especially we
combine it with reading, we are building

00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:49.784
up our capability to speak, but the
listening especially starts certain

00:05:49.784 --> 00:05:52.275
phrases sort of ringing in our brains.

00:05:52.515 --> 00:05:54.885
And we get to a point where
we want to try these out.

00:05:55.065 --> 00:05:56.474
We may use them wrong.

00:05:56.505 --> 00:06:03.030
We may fall flat on our face, but
we build up this well of things

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.580
that we want to say in the language,
because we've been listening a lot.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:12.659
And so the listening is sort of
the preparation for speaking, and

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:19.020
it's not only sort of the easiest
activity to do, it's very easy

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.030
to find an hour a day to listen.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.540
15 minutes here, 20 minutes
there you put together an hour.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.490
Uh, hopefully that triggers you
to go and read the same content if

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.110
you have access to the transcript.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.570
So it's a very easy activity to do.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:35.580
It's a fun activity.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.900
Listening comprehension is also a
major goal in language learning so that

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.240
the, the activity becomes the goal.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:51.659
Uh, not the medium is the message, but
you know, the activity of learning is the

00:06:51.659 --> 00:06:55.815
goal itself, because if we can achieve a
high degree of listening comprehension.

00:06:56.295 --> 00:07:01.125
We now have access to so much more in
the language that we can use to improve.

00:07:01.455 --> 00:07:05.565
This could be simply having meaningful
conversations with people because we

00:07:05.565 --> 00:07:07.485
understand more of what they're saying.

00:07:08.175 --> 00:07:11.475
It's very frustrating to be in a
conversation with someone where

00:07:11.475 --> 00:07:13.905
you struggle to say something,
and then you don't understand

00:07:13.905 --> 00:07:14.955
what they're saying back to you.

00:07:15.555 --> 00:07:19.034
Um, if we have good listening
comprehension, we can listen to podcasts.

00:07:19.275 --> 00:07:23.835
We can watch a Netflix films or series.

00:07:24.225 --> 00:07:29.055
So the listening comprehension, which
we achieved through lots of listening

00:07:29.505 --> 00:07:33.705
is not only the main activity it
becomes the goal because you get

00:07:33.705 --> 00:07:35.415
a sort of a multiplying effect.

00:07:35.655 --> 00:07:38.445
Once you achieve a certain level
of listening comprehension, you can

00:07:38.445 --> 00:07:42.885
listen to more compelling material,
which increases your ability to

00:07:42.885 --> 00:07:44.475
understand your listening comprehension.

00:07:44.745 --> 00:07:48.815
And it just gets you
on a sort of...some...

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.119
gives you momentum in your
language learning that will

00:07:51.119 --> 00:07:53.669
eventually lead to better fluency.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:58.530
So once you have enough of that
comprehension and you have taken advantage

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:03.030
of your listening comprehension to,
you know, access all of the kind of

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:07.320
content that's available on the internet,
listening and hopefully reading and

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:13.229
looking at words on LingQ or whatever,
once you are, you know, headed in

00:08:13.229 --> 00:08:15.039
that direction the speaking will come.

00:08:15.435 --> 00:08:19.335
And so that's why I always say, listen
before you speak, that's not to say

00:08:19.335 --> 00:08:22.995
you can't start speaking whenever you
want, but make sure that even as you

00:08:22.995 --> 00:08:28.545
start speaking, put a lot of emphasis
on listening because when you're,

00:08:28.545 --> 00:08:31.965
if you're not very, you know, at an
early stage, if your comprehension

00:08:31.965 --> 00:08:35.385
isn't very well developed and you're
struggling to say something, you're

00:08:35.385 --> 00:08:38.294
going to be mostly focusing on
trying to say what it is you have to.

00:08:39.015 --> 00:08:42.255
Whereas, if you're focusing enough
time on listening, you're building

00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:46.215
up that listening comprehension and
eventually you'll end up speaking better.

00:08:46.455 --> 00:08:49.815
So I just wanted to make that
point about listening someone

00:08:49.815 --> 00:08:53.625
similar to the discussion I had
in French a couple of weeks ago.

00:08:53.955 --> 00:08:54.885
Thank you for listening.

00:08:54.955 --> 00:08:55.365
Bye for now.

